**312.6 ms. 100% of it in Index Scan using orders\_created\_at\_idx on orders, which finds 1,104,080 rows through orders\_created\_at\_idx and its filter throws away 1,104,020. Nested Loop repeats Index Scan using orders\_created\_at\_idx on orders 20 times; that takes 100% of the loop's time.**

Planning 0.619 ms · Execution 312.6 ms · Buffers 1,109,606 pages, 100% from cache

| Share | Time | Node | Rows | Estimate | Buffers |
|---:|---:|---|---:|---:|---:|
| 0.058% | 0.180 ms | Nested Loop | 60 | 60 | 0 |
| 0.017% | 0.052 ms | &nbsp;&nbsp;↳ Index Only Scan using customers\_pkey on customers c | 20 | 20 | 3 |
| 0.026% | 0.080 ms | &nbsp;&nbsp;↳ Limit | 3 ×20 | 3 | 0 |
| 100% | 312.2 ms | &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;↳ Index Scan using orders\_created\_at\_idx on orders | 3 ×20 | 10 | 1,109,603 |

### Findings

- **High · ES005 Expensive nested-loop inner side:** Nested Loop repeats Index Scan using orders\_created\_at\_idx on orders 20 times; that takes 100% of the loop's time.
  - Inner loops: `20`
  - Inner side per loop: `15.6 ms`
  - Repeated work: `312.5 ms of the loop's 312.6 ms, 312.3 ms of it in the inner scans`
  - Rows removed per loop: `55,201 by the filter of Index Scan using orders_created_at_idx on orders`
  - **Action:** An index on orders (customer\_id) would turn each of the 20 inner scans into an index lookup.
- **High · ES006 Index scan that filters most rows:** Index Scan using orders\_created\_at\_idx on orders finds 1,104,080 rows through orders\_created\_at\_idx and its filter throws away 1,104,020.
  - Index: `orders_created_at_idx`
  - Filter: `(orders.customer_id = c.id)`
  - Rows removed by the filter: `55,201 of 55,204 found per loop`
  - Loops: `20`
  - **Action:** An index on orders that starts with customer\_id and continues with the columns of orders\_created\_at\_idx would let the index do the filtering.

### Advice

- **Index orders (customer\_id, created\_at)** (medium confidence): An index on orders (customer\_id, created\_at) would turn each of the 20 runs of Index Scan using orders\_created\_at\_idx on orders into an index lookup.
  ```sql
  CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY ON public.orders (customer_id, created_at);
  ```
  - Not connected to the database: existing indexes, the table's write load and the column statistics were not checked.
  - created\_at is taken from the name of orders\_created\_at\_idx, which Index Scan using orders\_created\_at\_idx on orders reads in order; plans do not list index columns.
